orig_q:
Solve the following pair of equations by substitution method:
\begin{align*}
7x - 15y &= 2 \quad (1) \\
x + 2y &= 3 \quad (2)
\end{align*}

pert_a:
\text{Step 1:} We pick either of the equations and write one variable in terms of the other. Let us consider Equation (2):
\begin{align*}
x + 2y &= 3 \\
x &= 3 - 2y \quad (3)
\end{align*}

\text{Step 2:} Substitute the value of \(x\) in Equation (1):
\begin{align*}
7(3 - 2y) - 15y &= 2 \\
21 - 14y - 15y &= 2 \\
21 - 29y &= 2 \\
-29y &= -19 \\
y &= \frac{19}{29}
\end{align*}

\text{Step 3:} Substituting this value of \(y\) in Equation (3), we get:
\begin{align*}
x &= 3 - 2\left(\frac{19}{29}\right) = \frac{49}{29}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{red}{\text{Therefore, the value of \(x\) is } x = \frac{49}{29}.}

 We have
\[
\text{L.H.S.} = 3 \sin \frac{\pi}{6} \sec \frac{\pi}{3} - 4 \sin \frac{5\pi}{6} \cot \frac{\pi}{4}
\]
\[
= 3 \times \frac{1}{2} \times 2 - 4 \sin \left( \frac{\pi - \frac{\pi}{6}}{1} \right) \times 1 = 3 - 4 \sin \textcolor{red}{\frac{\pi}{3}}
\]
\[
= 3 - 4 \times \frac{1}{2} = 1 = \text{R.H.S.}
\]

 We have 
\[
4x + i(3x - y) = 3 + i(-6)
\]
\textcolor{red}{Solving for \( x \) and \( y \) simultaneously, we get }
\[
\textcolor{red}{x = \frac{3}{4} \quad \text{and} \quad y = \frac{33}{4}.}
\]
\textcolor{red}{Equating the real and the imaginary parts, we get }
\[
\textcolor{red}{4x = 3, \quad 3x - y = -6.}
\]

orig_q:
Find the value of 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \( 2^{-3} \)
    \item \( \frac{1}{3^{-2}} \)
\end{enumerate}

pert_a:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \( 2^{-3} = 2 \times (-3) = \textcolor{red}{-6} \)
    \item \( \frac{1}{3^{-2}} = \frac{1}{3 \times (-2)} = \textcolor{red}{\frac{1}{-6}} \)
\end{enumerate}

